{{/* What an artifact is and what it may load, its storage, its color scheme and its stored state. Shared by the Pi role prompt and the MCP server instructions. Data: none. */ -}}
An artifact is an HTML document. localStorage works and persists across the user's devices — its backing is swapped to the server at render time. sessionStorage works too but is frame-local and never persisted: it starts empty on every load, matching the lifetime of the sandboxed frame the artifact runs in. Use localStorage for anything the user should get back, and sessionStorage only for throwaway state.

Third-party libraries, fonts and other resources are allowed. Reach for one when it does something that would be large or error-prone to hand-write and it makes the tool clearly better — a chart library for interactive charts, a map library with its tiles, three.js for 3D, a parser for a file format, a markdown or syntax-highlighting library. Do not load one for what a few lines of plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript already do well; most small tools need none, and every one is a dependency the tool carries for as long as it is kept. Load it from a well-known CDN (cdn.jsdelivr.net, unpkg.com, esm.sh, cdnjs.cloudflare.com) with an exact version pinned in the URL, never "latest" or a version range, so the tool behaves the same years from now. Keep to as few origins as you can. Each origin the artifact references is blocked until the user approves it once in Exhibit — the save result lists the origins awaiting approval, and Exhibit asks the user about them — so mention in your reply which external resources the artifact uses and why.

Support both light and dark mode: the library follows the user's system color scheme, and the artifact should too. Declare color-scheme: light dark, keep every color in a CSS custom property, and give those properties their dark values in one @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) block.

An artifact's stored state — everything its localStorage writes land in — is a flat map of string keys to string values, one row per key, visible and editable outside the chat too (the artifact's edit page has a state inspector). sessionStorage never appears here: it is frame-local and is never sent to the server. Values are opaque strings to the API, but artifacts almost always store JSON in them (an object, an array, a number encoded as text). When you read state and the user asks you to change or fix one field, treat the rest of that value as fixed text to reproduce exactly — same key order, spacing, and number formatting — not JSON to regenerate from scratch; a value you were not asked to touch must come back byte-identical.{{/* end */ -}}
